# Stage 16 — bidirectionality verification log (tickets #83 #84 #85 #86)

Discipline per axis: PASS (fix in, test green) → real break (revert the fix,
verify the test genuinely FAILS naming the wrong value) → restore (fix back,
green). Quoted assertion text is the verbatim failure the broken code produced.

Baseline (fix-free trunk, worktree): 759 tests / 31 files green; `npm run check`
green.

---

## Axis A — #85 Blueprint machine numbers 1-based

Fix: `src/ui/Blueprint.tsx:204` `{i}` → `{i + 1}`.
Test: `src/ui/smoke.test.tsx` › Blueprint › "labels machine rects 1-based
(1..N), not 0-based (#85)" — asserts the two rendered `bp-machine-label` texts
equal `["1", "2"]` on the real Smelter ×2 solve.

- PASS: fix in → `Tests  1 passed`.
- BREAK: reverted `{i + 1}` → `{i}`; test FAILED:
  > `AssertionError: expected [ '0', '1' ] to deeply equal [ '1', '2' ]`
  The break reproduces exactly Michael's report — a 0-based label makes the
  first rect wear "0" and the "after machine m" mark read one rect early.
- RESTORE: fix back → `Tests  1 passed`.

Drift-hunt (assumption #2): grepped every `*.test.*` for a 0-based blueprint
label content pin (`bp-machine-label`, `>0</text>`) — NONE exists (the only
`machine-label` matches are the schematic band-mark COUNT in smoke.test.tsx).
The new pin is additive, not a flipped existing pin. Verified.

